    "stem": "The diagram shows Yamini's square courtyard tile. Its area is $1296\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{2}$. She writes the prime factorisation $1296 = 2^{4} \\times 3^{4}$ and asks whether $1296$ is a perfect square and, if it is, what its square root is.\n\nWhich claim is correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "$1296$ is a perfect square because the primes split into two identical groups $2^{2} \\times 3^{2}$ and $2^{2} \\times 3^{2}$, so $\\sqrt{1296} = 36$.",
      "B": "Report the words factor pair as the square root of $1296$.",
      "C": "The square root $36$ produces the prime factorisation $2^{4} \\times 3^{4}$, so she starts from $36$ instead of pairing the primes.",
      "D": "Read $1296$ as a perfect cube and take its cube root instead of pairing the prime factors."
    },
